State the palette invariant instead of counting palettes by hand

CI failed on one test in the first commit of this branch:

    tests/test_aaos_icon_b10_contract.py::test_icon_stroke_and_foreground_follow_both_theme_palettes
    assert palettes.count('["AaosIvoryBrush"]') == 2
    assert 3 == 2

The assertion was written when two palettes existed. The cosmic theme layer added a third -
深空主题 / DeepSpace - and moved Apply() from a two-way choice to a switch over three tables, so
each palette defines the ivory brush the icons bind to and there are now three definitions. The
behaviour is correct; the assertion described the palette count rather than the rule it was testing.

It now states the rule: one ivory-brush definition per palette table, whatever number of tables
exists, read from the declarations themselves. That keeps the original intent and will not need
editing again when a fourth palette appears - while still failing if a palette forgets the brush.

Palette tables verified to be structurally identical before changing the assertion (34 lines each),
so a complete third palette was added rather than a partial one.

The test file is one of the files outside the six the branch already touched, which is why the
first commit's local run did not catch it: the six affected contract files pass, and this one is not
among them.

CI is the authority for this fix - the local run of the full suite also reports six diagnostics and
release-manifest failures, but those are the known host-environment class documented in
AAOS-ERRORS-BLOCKERS-20261001.md (one asserts no absolute-path marker while the worktree path itself
contains "D:\") and CI reported none of them.
